RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
Office of City Clerk
October 25, 2010
The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne met in regular session on this date beginning
at 6:00 p.m. in Council Chambers.  Present were: MAYOR - Richard L. Kaysen; COUNCIL
MEMBERS - Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case, Mr. Collins, Dr. Rinne, Mr. Pierson,
and Mr. Spiker. Absent:  Mr. Valdez.  Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk; Barb Dorr, City
Treasurer, and Dan White, City Attorney.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.
Consent Agenda. (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to be
routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion.  There will be no separate
discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and support by two
other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be considered in its
normal sequence on the agenda.) Mr. Collins moved to approve Consent Agenda items, seconded
by Mr. Spiker.  Motion carried. Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.
[CA]
Minutes from Regular Meeting of the Governing Body on October 11, 2010.  Motion (per
Consent Agenda) carried.
[CA]
Minutes from Vouchers Meeting of the Governing Body on October 18, 2010.  Motion (per
Consent Agenda) carried.
Mr. Brown moved to approve payment of vouchers as presented, seconded by Mr. Spiker.
Upon inquiry, Mayor Kaysen, City Treasurer Barb Dorr and Planning Services Director Matt Ashby
provided information available pertaining to a purchase of an Apple iPad, iTunes for software
applications, case, and protection plan in the amount of $691.01 included in a voucher payable to
VISA/UMB.  It was noted the purchase was made by the Metropolitan Planning Organization
(MPO) with federal funding and that the equipment, which provides more flexibility and portability
than a laptop computer, and software will be utilized for the MPO on-site Employment Data
Collection Project.  Dr. Rinne moved to divide the question to allow for separate action on the
purchase of the iTunes component in the amount of $54.01, seconded by Ms. Ash.  Motion carried.
Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.  Dr. Rinne moved to postpone action on
the payment of $54.01 for iTunes for one week (to Nov. 1, 2010), seconded by Ms. Ash.
Comments followed regarding the purpose of the iPad purchase and what applications software was
acquired through iTunes.  Ms. Dorr advised of payment time frame options relative to the vendor’s
invoice due date, and Mr. Ashby clarified that he had confirmed with the MPO Director that the
programs downloaded via iTunes involved word processing, spreadsheets and presentation
programs.  Motion to postpone failed.  Voting “yes” – Ms. Ash, Dr. Rinne and Mr. Spiker.  Voting
“no” – Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case, Mr. Collins, Mr. Pierson, and Mayor Kaysen.  Mr.
Collins moved to approve payment of the Apple iTunes portion of the purchase order, seconded by
Mr. Brown. Discussion followed on the purpose and necessity of purchasing an iPad and affiliated
software items.  Motion to approve carried.  Voting “yes” – Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case, Mr.
Collins, Mr. Pierson, and Mayor Kaysen.  Voting “no” – Ms. Ash, Dr. Rinne and Mr. Spiker.
Discussion on remaining vouchers followed.  Upon inquiry regarding a voucher payable to Service
First Carpet Cleaning ($450.00), carpet cleaning at Taco John’s Event Center (TJEC) due to smoke
```

### Page 2 of 6

```text
damage resulting from the fire at the Hitching Post Inn and whether the cost might be recoverable
through insurance, City Attorney Dan White advised a claim could be asserted and stated the matter
is being researched with regard to potential coverage through the Hitching Post’s insurance carrier.
Upon inquiry whether any other damage was incurred due to the fire, Rick Parish, Parks and
Recreation Director, advised there was a lot of external debris due to flooding in the parking lot
from fire extinguishing efforts and indicated he had compiled a list and submitted it to the Mayor
for possible recouping of clean-up expenses through insurance. Regarding inquiry on a refund for
products ordered from Howies that were never shipped by the company, Ms. Dorr explained this
was a pre-charge expense placed on a City credit purchasing card and since the item was not
received the company issued a credit in the amount of $126.67.  Motion to approve remaining
vouchers carried. Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present with the exception of
Dr. Rinne voting “no”.
Mayor Kaysen opened a public hearing for a transfer of ownership for a restaurant liquor
license application filed with the City Clerk’s Office for Soo Hyun Choi, LLC d/b/a Tokyo Bowl,
1829 Dell Range Boulevard, Cheyenne, WY. (License currently held by Iron Chefs, Inc. d/b/a
Tokyo Bowl, 1829 Dell Range Boulevard, Cheyenne, WY.)  Upon inquiry, City Clerk Carol
Intlekofer clarified new owners of the business are requesting the transfer of ownership and
explained there is no change to the existing alcohol dispensing room.  There being no further
comments or discussion and purpose of the public hearing having been met, Mayor Kaysen closed
the hearing and referred the application to the Finance Committee.
“An Ordinance annexing to the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming, a parcel of land being situated
in the SW1/4 of Section 25, T.14N., R.66W., 6th P.M., Laramie County, Wyoming (generally
located north of Pershing Boulevard, between Whitney Road and Christensen Road)” -- referred to
Public Services Committee (sponsor - Ms. Ash).
“A Resolution re-appropriating the fiscal year 2011 budget of the City of Cheyenne
according to the provisions of W.S. 16-4-112 & 16-4-113.” Upon query to members of the
governing body by Mayor Kaysen regarding a brief statement he wished to make concerning the
re-appropriation, and there being no objection raised, Mayor Kaysen provided information on a
proposed employee recognition payment included in the re-appropriation.  He explained the
intent was to recognize full-time employees for their efforts this past calendar year and stated the
payment was not intended to include the Mayor or members of the governing body. Mayor
Kaysen noted other items included in the proposed re-appropriation and concluded his statement
by indicating he would have no objection if the employee recognition payment was not part of
the re-appropriation.  Reporting for Finance Committee, Mr. Brown moved to adopt, seconded by
Mr. Pierson.  Mr. Brown moved to amend by substitute, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Mr. Brown
clarified the substitute removes the line item for the employee recognition payment, which will
change the total re-appropriation amount.  Planning Services Director Matt Ashby, Public Works
Director Jim Elias, City Treasurer Barb Dorr and Mayor Kaysen provided information in
response to inquiry and discussion that included the following topics: Request for an additional
planner position in the Building/Development Office to assist with increased workload involving
building permits and site plans; staff reductions, reorganization and reassignment of duties in
several City departments due to the reduction in workforce and voluntary early retirements
occurring earlier in the year; effect of reduced staffing levels on customer service, maintenance
of fleet vehicles and equipment (safety) and high volume areas (sanitation, street and alley); part-
```

### Page 3 of 6

```text
time employment services being received by the Public Works Department by a former director
of the department; staff reassignments made to assist with coverage; outsourcing of certain Fleet
Maintenance repair services and changes to the division’s preventative vehicles/equipment
maintenance schedules; re-appropriation line item involving juvenile justice grants; staffing
levels and review of same prior to and during the Fiscal Year 2011-2012 budget process; time
frame involved in the deferred $1,000,000 payment to the Board of Public Utilities, and
administrative process utilized when determining whether to fill a vacated staff position.  Motion
to amend carried.  Voting “yes” –  all members of the governing body present. Additional
comment of concern was made regarding staffing levels.  Main motion as amended by substitute
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.  (#5247)
“A Resolution authorizing the Mayor and the City Clerk to sign a Final Plat for Teton
Lumber, a replat of vacated portions of Lots 3, 4 & 5, Block 29; and Lots 1 & 2, Block 30; and
McComb Avenue, Original City of Cheyenne; and a portion of Lot 3, Section 36, T.14N.,
R.67W., 6th P.M., Cheyenne, Wyoming (located at the northwest corner of the intersection of
Cribbon Avenue and W. 27th Street).”  Reporting for Public Services Committee, Ms. Ash
moved to adopt with staff recommendations #1 through 4, seconded by Mr. Collins. Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.  (#5248)
“A Resolution authorizing the Mayor and the City Clerk to sign a Final Plat for The
Bluffs, 8th Filing, situated in a portion of the SW¼ NW¼ of Section 21, T.14N., R.66W., 6th
P.M., Cheyenne, Wyoming (located east of and adjacent to Ogden Road, between Apache Street
and Plain View Road).”  Kelly Hafner, Benchmark Engineers, advised he was involved with
investigating drainage issues for the developer, United Western Corporation, affiliated with the
project, and submitted a map diagram (on file with City Clerk’s office) depicting a proposed
regional detention facility for the area.  Mr. Hafner referenced recommendations relative to the
preliminary plat; reviewed areas contained in the map diagram, and proposed consideration of a
regional drainage detention facility vs. small pocket detention area(s).  He discussed constraints
of the area based on his research of drainage flow direction, topography and run-off volumes, and
the difficulty of extending Plainview Road to Converse Ave. due to drainage flows and City
water main issues; explained logistics involving a regional facility vs. multiple smaller detention
ponds, and noted affiliated maintenance responsibility issues.  Discussion included the following
topics:  Potential interim detention lot, depicted as Lot 10, Block 11 on the plat map, and
developer construction options; run-off volumes draining into the City storm sewer system;
issues and concerns involving the proposed regional detention facility, which would currently
include property in the county (not within City limits) over small pocket areas; physical
characteristics of Lot 10, Block 11; improvements to drainage system, and extension of
Plainview Road to Converse Ave.  Frank Cole, United Western Corporation, developer, provided
information on water drainage in the area; cited other development projects involving drainage
issues, and stated as development to the north advances the intent would be for the area to be
annexed.  Doug Vetter, City Engineer, stated the recommendation of development staff is that
Lot 10, Block 11 be reserved for drainage on the final plat; not necessarily dedicated as drainage.
He noted potential concerns with the location and design of the regional facility, and preclusion
of the extension of Plainview Road which could affect the area’s transportation network.  Ms.
Ash moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Ms. Ash moved to postpone for two weeks (to
Nov. 8, 2010), seconded by Mr. Collins. It was advised the postponement would allow for
continuing discussion on future road connectivity and drainage.  Comments followed regarding
```

### Page 4 of 6

```text
drainage and Plainview Road extension concerns; reserve vs. dedicate Lot 10, Block 11 for
drainage; detention area(s) maintenance responsibility, and small local detention areas generally.
Upon inquiry, City Attorney Dan White explained the recommendation noted by Mr. Vetter is
being requested to be placed on the plat to ensure drainage issues for this specific plat are
addressed by reserving the lot for detention purposes.  He added that the developer may desire to
retain the lot in private ownership and that the City’s intent is to reserve the lot, not dedicate it to
the City.  Mr. Cole stated his advocacy for a  regional detention pond to be maintained by the
City, and noted liability risks to property owners involving ownership of small detention pond
areas.  He spoke in opposition to the staff recommendation to “tag” Lot 10, Block 11 as reserved
and stated his intention is not to even use Lot 10 for drainage. Comments followed in support of
regional detention ponds, connectivity of east-west roads for future use, local elementary school
traffic, and problems with incremental developments.  Motion to postpone failed.  Voting “yes” –
Dr. Rinne.  Voting “no” – Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case, Mr. Collins, Mr. Pierson,
Mr. Spiker and Mayor Kaysen.  Mr. Collins moved to amend to include staff recommendation
#1, seconded by Mr. Brown, which reads: “Indicate Lot 10, Block 11 as an area reserved for
storm water detention on final plat map”.  Discussion followed on a regional detention facility vs.
interim local detention ponds; potential route alternatives to provide connector street to Converse
Ave.; physical natural depth of Lot 10, Block 11 relative to drainage use; proximity of detention
facility/pond(s) to an elementary school in the area and concerns with traffic and safety of
students; use of Lot 10, Block 11 for drainage during plat development; need for future
discussion regarding the regional detention facility, and unattractiveness of local detention areas.
During discussion on alternate routes to extend street traffic to Converse Ave., Mr. Cole provided
information concerning the Water Tank Bluff street area.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Hafner, provided
information on the depth of Lot 10, Block 11, and size of the proposed regional detention facility,
stating it would be a “bare minimal size” with a gravity drain system.  Motion to amend with
staff recommendation #1 carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present
with the exception of Ms. Ash, Ms. Broyles, Dr. Rinne and Mayor Kaysen voting “no”.  Upon
inquiry concerning whether a barrier or fencing of side slopes of Lot 10, Block 11 would be
installed, Mr. Hafner stated the side slopes do not require a barrier, and at this time no barrier or
fencing is planned and the area would be open.  Main motion as amended carried.  Voting “yes”
– all members of the governing body present with the exception of Dr. Rinne and Mayor Kaysen
voting “no”. (#5249)
[CA]
Agreement between the City of Cheyenne, Office of Youth Alternatives, and Laramie
County, for juvenile probation services. Motion (per Consent Agenda) carried.  (#5603)
[CA]
Contract between the City of Cheyenne, Office of Youth Alternatives, and the State of
Wyoming, Department of Family Services, for intensive support mentoring program for youth in
the juvenile court system.  Motion (per Consent Agenda) carried.  (#5604)
[CA]
Employment contract between the Cheyenne Police Department and Alicia ‘Lisa’ Scholz
for suicide and substance abuse prevention program management services (Core Prevention
